WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:20.574
As soon as Ben Gunn saw the flag above the cabin in the stockade, he grabbed me by the arm. "Ah, your friends are there," he said.

2
00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:23.892
"It's probably the mutineers," I answered.

3
00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:30.861
"No, Long John would fly the Jolly Roger, the skull and crossbones flag. That's your friends.

4
00:00:30.862 --> 00:00:38.088
There's been a fight, and I'll bet your friends are now ashore in the old stockade that Flint built years ago."

5
00:00:38.089 --> 00:00:41.876
"Well, then," I said, "I should hurry and join them."

6
00:00:41.877 --> 00:00:46.103
"If anyone needs me, they can find me where we met today.

7
00:00:46.104 --> 00:00:52.535
But whoever comes to find me must carry something white and they must come alone.

8
00:00:52.536 --> 00:00:57.694
If they ask why, tell them, 'Ben Gunn has reasons of his own.'"

9
00:00:57.695 --> 00:01:01.367
"I understand," I said. "Is that all?"

10
00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:06.369
He nodded. "They can find me from about noon to about six o'clock."

11
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.967
"Good."

12
00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:15.448
"And, Jim, if you see Long John, don't tell him you saw me! Please don't!"

13
00:01:15.449 --> 00:01:21.827
"I wouldn't―," I started to say, but suddenly, we were interrupted by a loud, sharp noise.

14
00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:25.021
A cannonball came tearing through the trees.

15
00:01:25.022 --> 00:01:29.781
The next moment, Ben Gunn and I ran off in different directions.

16
00:01:29.782 --> 00:01:33.969
For the next hour, balls kept crashing through the woods.

17
00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:40.420
I moved from place to place, always pursued—or so it seemed—by those terrifying missiles.

18
00:01:40.421 --> 00:01:46.568
Feeling brave, I finally crept down among the shoreside trees to see what was going on.

19
00:01:46.569 --> 00:01:55.884
The Hispaniola was still where she'd been anchored, but sure enough, the Jolly Roger—the flag of piracy—was flying from her mast.

20
00:01:55.885 --> 00:02:03.621
I lay for a while, watching as two pirates on the shore near the stockade demolished our poor, sunken rowboat.

21
00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:10.005
Then I crawled closer to the mouth of the river, where a great fire glowed among the trees.

22
00:02:10.006 --> 00:02:15.646
One of the shore boats kept going to and from the ship, as the pirates shouted drunkenly to each other.

23
00:02:15.647 --> 00:02:20.638
"Don't forget the sea biscuits!" hollered one man on the beach.

24
00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:26.452
"Quiet, swab!" yelled another, rowing toward the ship. "I want more rum!"

25
00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:30.725
I got up and, keeping out of sight, headed toward the stockade.

26
00:02:30.726 --> 00:02:38.666
Soon I saw an isolated rock rising high up from among the low bushes. Strangely, it was white.

27
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:42.009
"Could that be Ben Gunn's white rock?" I asked myself.

28
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.755
"That might be the place where he keeps his boat."

29
00:02:44.756 --> 00:02:48.593
I continued walking through the woods and was soon at the stockade;

30
00:02:48.594 --> 00:02:52.698
I scrambled over the fence with high hopes that my friends would be there.

31
00:02:52.699 --> 00:02:57.128
"Jim!" the doctor shouted when he saw me, and welcomed me warmly.

32
00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:05.242
As I told my story, I looked around and noticed that the log house was made of pine tree trunks and had a floor about a foot above the sand.

33
00:03:05.243 --> 00:03:11.886
There was a porch, and under it, a little spring was bubbling up into a big iron pot with the bottom knocked out.

34
00:03:11.887 --> 00:03:19.648
Inside, a stone slab formed a corner hearth, and a big, rusty iron grate contained a smoky fire.

35
00:03:19.649 --> 00:03:25.116
Gray, the new man, had his face bandaged because of a cut he'd gotten from the mutineers.

36
00:03:25.117 --> 00:03:30.472
And poor Tom Redruth was laid out along the wall under the Union Jack.

37
00:03:30.473 --> 00:03:36.012
To keep us busy and our spirits up, Captain Smollett gave everyone something to do.

38
00:03:36.013 --> 00:03:40.610
"Men," the captain said, "we're all tired, but we can't just sit around.

39
00:03:40.611 --> 00:03:46.358
Gray and Hunter will collect firewood. The squire and Joyce will dig Redruth's grave.

40
00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:50.954
The doctor will be our cook, and Jim, you'll be the lookout at the door."

41
00:03:50.955 --> 00:03:55.600
A bit later, the doctor came to the door for air and tilted his head toward me.

42
00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:59.503
"Tell me again about Ben Gunn," he said.

43
00:03:59.504 --> 00:04:03.911
"Well," I replied, "I'm not sure he's sane."

44
00:04:03.912 --> 00:04:07.663
"If you have any doubt, he probably is," said the doctor.

45
00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:16.339
"You can't expect a man who's been on a deserted island for three years to appear as sane as you or I. You said he had a craving for cheese?"

46
00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.297
"Yes, sir, cheese," I answered.

47
00:04:19.298 --> 00:04:28.905
"Well, Jim," he said with a laugh, "inside my medicine bag, I carry a piece of Parmesan cheese—a cheese made in Italy, very nutritious.

48
00:04:28.906 --> 00:04:32.896
That's going to be for our friend Ben Gunn!"

49
00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:37.334
Before supper, we buried old Tom in the sand and said a prayer.

50
00:04:37.335 --> 00:04:41.430
Then we collected firewood, but not enough to satisfy the captain.

51
00:04:41.431 --> 00:04:44.985
"We'll get back to this tomorrow when we're more rested," he said.

52
00:04:44.986 --> 00:04:48.746
"But now, let's eat and then make some plans."

53
00:04:48.747 --> 00:04:57.377
We ate a bit of pork and each had a good, stiff glass of brandy, and then the doctor, the squire, and the captain huddled together in a corner.

54
00:04:57.378 --> 00:05:00.823
"We'll starve to death long before help comes," declared Smollett.

55
00:05:00.824 --> 00:05:03.884
"Our best hope is to kill all the pirates."

56
00:05:03.885 --> 00:05:09.416
"We've already reduced their number from nineteen to fifteen," noted the squire.

57
00:05:09.417 --> 00:05:12.629
"And others are wounded," the captain added.

58
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:18.123
"Well, we have two strong allies—rum and the climate," the doctor said.

59
00:05:18.124 --> 00:05:24.383
"Listen to them! We're half a mile away and can still hear them singing late into the night.

60
00:05:24.384 --> 00:05:30.044
Making camp in the marsh, without medicine, half will be sick in a week." He frowned.

61
00:05:30.045 --> 00:05:36.279
"So, hopefully," he continued, "they'll be glad to return to the ship and get back to sea again."

62
00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:41.111
"That's the first ship I ever lost," Captain Smollett said sadly.

63
00:05:41.112 --> 00:05:46.355
We all went to bed then, and after a great deal of tossing, I slept soundly.

64
00:05:46.356 --> 00:05:50.473
In the morning, I was awakened by the sound of excited voices.

65
00:05:50.474 --> 00:05:54.304
"There's a flag of truce!" someone exclaimed.

66
00:05:54.305 --> 00:05:59.103
Then another, with a cry of surprise, said, "It's Silver himself!"

67
00:05:59.104 --> 00:06:08.307
I jumped up, rubbed my eyes, and ran to a musket hole in the wall.
